{"name":"Pansy Petal","hex":"#5f4561","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pansy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cultivated flowering plant, derived by hybridization within species Viola tricolor.","A deep purple colour, like that of the pansy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wimpy; spineless; feeble.","Of a deep purple colour, like that of the pansy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pansy"},{"term":"petal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the component parts of the corolla of a flower. It applies particularly, but not necessarily only, when the corolla consists of separate parts, that is when the petals are not connately fused. Petals are often brightly colored.","The labia (majora)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spread out from, like the petals of a flower"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/petal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pansy","blocks":[{"text":"The garden pansy is a type of polychromatic large-flowered hybrid plant cultivated as a garden flower. It is derived by hybridization from several species in the section Melanium of the genus Viola, particularly V. tricolor, a wildflower of Europe and western Asia known as heartsease. It is sometimes known as V. tricolor var. hortensis, but this scientific name is suspect. While V. tricolor var. hortensis Groenland & Rümpler is a synonym of Viola × wittrockiana, V. tricolor var. hortensis DC. refers to a horticultural variety of wild pansy that had been illustrated in Flora Danica in 1777 before the existence of Viola × wittrockiana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/PansyScan_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-PansyScan_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pansy","wikidata":"Q12861234"},{"title":"Petal","blocks":[{"text":"Petals are modified leaves that form an inner whorl surrounding the reproductive parts of flowers. They are often brightly coloured or unusually shaped to attract pollinators. All of the petals of a flower are collectively known as the corolla."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Mature_flower_diagram.svg/330px-Mature_flower_diagram.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petal","wikidata":"Q107412"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Pansy_Flower.jpg/500px-Pansy_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Pansy_Flower.jpg/500px-Pansy_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pansy flower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APansy_Flower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Purple_and_black_viola_cultivar_in_Toronto.jpg/500px-Purple_and_black_viola_cultivar_in_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Purple_and_black_viola_cultivar_in_Toronto.jpg/500px-Purple_and_black_viola_cultivar_in_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pansy displaying the two upper overlapping petals, the two side petals, and the single bottom petal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APurple_and_black_viola_cultivar_in_Toronto.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pansy_bi_colour.jpg/500px-Pansy_bi_colour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pansy_bi_colour.jpg/1280px-Pansy_bi_colour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Pansy_bi_colour.